Once again - Obvious does NOT equal true.  The above is an
example of something that is obvious that is false.  Saving
does NOT make the game ascendable anything like 100% of
the time.  In fact saving makes it almost assured that you
will make a strategic mistake several levels before that results
in an untenable situation.  To get out of that you'd need to
store a save file for each and every level you complete and
keep backing out further and further into the past.

Save scumming did work for me to learn tactics.  I learned
melee, retreat, missile use, the skill enhancement system
and such.  I did become somewhat a better player while I was
in that phase.  But eventually it stopped working no matter
how obvious it might be.  Obvious does not equal true.

Once I exitted my save scumming phase years ago I started
learning strategy.  When the price of losing a character is
starting over from scratch I started not only learning the
tactical lesson of what I'd done wrong since entering a level
but the strategic reason of how I ended to in an untenable
situation in the first place.

Recently I've been losing characters to trap doors in the
mines.  I don't seem to have any problem falling through one,
finding the up stairs, returning to my pet, resuming progress.
I do seem to have a problem with falling through a second
trap door and ending up two levels deeper than I intended.
There's too much chance of ending up in a crowd of monsters
and not being able to find the upstairs in time to retreat.  The
mines have to many dead ends that can end up fitting crowds.

I've been pondering why I've been getting into this danger
recently.  I'm slightly more aggressive at descending the
mines than I used to be.  I used to explore a level of the
mines, a level of the main dungeon, back and forth until I
found Sokoban.  Do Sokoban just before mine town or at
least before descending below mine town.  Lately I've just
been descending into mine town and depending on being
able to retreat up the stairs before the level is cleared out.

That's a strategic lesson I never could have learned while
save scumming because I could have restored to the point
before I encountered the two trap doors in a row.


